## Why This Is Asked
Tests a novel fixed-gap non-crossing pairing constraint, a natural extension of classic non-crossing matching problems, while probing DP state design and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes with fixed pair distance
- Non-crossing/disjoint constraint handling
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Space/time optimization trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxFixedGapPairs(A, d, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(false));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
      if (t > 0 && i - d - 1 >= 0) {
        const cand = dp[i - d - 1][t - 1] + A[i - 1] * A[i - d - 1];
        if (cand > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = cand;
          take[i][t] = true;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  const pairs = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    if (take[i][t]) {
      pairs.push([i - d, i]); // 1-based indices of the pair
      i = i - d - 1;
      t--;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }
  pairs.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], pairs };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a non-trivial DP over a linear sequence with a non-crossing constraint and reconstruction of the chosen pairs. It balances readability with the need to optimize across many left endpoints efficiently.

## Key Concepts

- Non-crossing pairings on a line
- DP by right endpoint with reconstruction
- Time/space trade-offs; potential 1D optimization for i dimension

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxNonCrossingPairs(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const take = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      let best = i>0 ? dp[i-1][t] : 0;
      let bestJ = -1;
      for(let j=0;j<i;j++){
        const left = (j>=1) ? dp[j-1][t-1] : (t-1===0 ? 0 : NEG);
        const val = left + Math.abs(A[i]-A[j]);
        if(val > best){ best = val; bestJ = j; }
      }
      dp[i][t] = best;
      take[i][t] = bestJ;
    }
  }
  // reconstruction sketch (omitted for brevity)
  return { value: dp[n-1][k], pairs: [] };
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you enforce exactly k pairs instead of at most k?
- Can you optimize memory to O(n) or derive a monotone queue optimization for certain A distributions?

## Why This Is Asked
Offers a new DP angle: path with exact turns, combining grid path DP with turn accounting. Tests deriving a tight state space, base cases, and reconstruction, mirroring routing-like constraints in real systems.

## Key Concepts
- Grid DP with an extra dimension for turns
- Two direction states to capture turns
- Reconstruction via backpointers
- Careful base/edge-case handling (start cell, negative infinities)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithTurns(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  // 4D DP: [i][j][t][d]
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: m}, () =>
    Array.from({length: K+1}, () => [NEG, NEG])));
  dp[0][0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;
        // last move right
        if (j > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][0] = Math.max(
            (dp[i][j-1][t][0] !== NEG ? dp[i][j-1][t][0] : NEG),
            (t > 0 && dp[i-1][j][t-1][1] !== NEG ? dp[i-1][j][t-1][1] : NEG)
          ) + grid[i][j];
        }
        // last move down
        if (i > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t][1] = Math.max(
            (dp[i-1][j][t][1] !== NEG ? dp[i-1][j][t][1] : NEG),
            (t > 0 && dp[i][j-1][t-1][0] !== NEG ? dp[i][j-1][t-1][0] : NEG)
          ) + grid[i][j];
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return Math.max(dp[n-1][m-1][K][0], dp[n-1][m-1][K][1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to allow at most k turns and also maximize path length if grid values included negative numbers?
- How would you optimize space to O(nmK) memory or better, and what would be the trade-offs for reconstruction?

## Why This Problem Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of dynamic programming on prefixes with a non-crossing pairing constraint. The core challenge is encoding the no-crossing condition by considering the last pair ending at position i with its left partner p.

## Key Concepts
- **DP on prefixes**: Extra dimension tracks the number of pairs formed
- **Non-crossing constraint**: Enforced by pairing i with p < i and using dp[p-1][t-1], which ensures all pairs lie completely to the left of p
- **Reconstruction**: Requires storing backpointers to output the actual pairs
- **Complexity**: Time O(n²k), Space O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPairs(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(NEG));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
      prev[i][t] = { skip: true };

      for (let p = 1; p < i; p++) {
        const val = dp[p - 1][t - 1] + A[p - 1] * A[i - 1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = { skip: false, p };
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const pairs = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    if (prev[i][t].skip) {
      i--;
    } else {
      const p = prev[i][t].p;
      pairs.push([p, i]);
      i = p - 1;
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], pairs: pairs.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to transform per-element cost into a standard max-sum DP for exactly k non-overlapping subarrays and to reconstruct endpoints. It emphasizes exact-k and non-overlap constraints common in analytics problems.

## Key Concepts
- Transform to B[i] = A[i] - c and reduce to max-sum of k non-overlapping subarrays
- DP states: local[i][t] for subarray ending at i; glob[i][t] for best so far with t subarrays
- Recurrences and reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(B, k) {
  const n = B.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const local = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const glob  = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  local[0][0] = 0; glob[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      local[i][t] = Math.max(local[i-1][t] + B[i-1], glob[i-1][t-1] + B[i-1]);
      glob[i][t] = Math.max(glob[i-1][t], local[i][t]);
    }
  }
  return {max: glob[n][k], endpoints: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the exact endpoints efficiently?
- How would you adapt if at-most-k subarrays were allowed instead of exactly k?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests deriving a clean DP where subarray cost depends on endpoints, not internal aggregates, and requires careful index handling and backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- DP state dp[i][t] for first i elements into t blocks
- Transition over split point p with endpoint cost |A[p+1]-A[i]| 
- Backpointer reconstruction for endpoints
- Time O(n^2 k), space O(nk) (can optimize with rolling arrays)

## Code Example
```javascript
function minEndPointPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = Math.abs(A[0] - A[i]);
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t - 1; i < n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 2; p <= i - 1; p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t - 1] + Math.abs(A[p + 1] - A[i]);
        if (val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let endpoints = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = par[i][t];
    endpoints.unshift([p + 1, i]);
    i = p; t--;
  }
  return { cost: dp[n - 1][k], endpoints };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if cost were |A[l]-A[r]|^2 or involved a different function?
- Can you optimize to O(nk) time with monotone queue tricks if the cost has structure?

